fix(owner): an unreadable argv is 'unknown', not 'stranger'

Neuromancer's pid-1 sample applied here: proc_pidinfo(1) is EPERM while ps sees
launchd, so a two-valued in/out test reads a live process as gone. The same
shape lived in _reclaim_by_profile, where every way of failing to claim
(no profile, nobody listening, argv unreadable, another profile) collapsed into
one "recorded process is not alive" and came out as "stranger".

"stranger" is a claim of evidence, not a claim of ignorance. The four ways of
not claiming now separate: no-listener and other-profile stay "stranger"
(positive evidence of absence / of somebody else); unreadable becomes
"unknown" with reason "no evidence either way".

Caught on the way in: the first cut of this change also broke the positive
answer -- the helper now returns a 3-tuple, so an "if claim:" elsewhere was
always true and a recycled pid was reported as mine. tests/test_owner.py
test_recycled_pid_is_stranger caught it. 785 tests green.
